        "expected": "I want to spend a summer building software that real people depend on daily, and Hudl's product has that property: coaches and athletes use it as part of their actual routine, so a bug or a slow feature shows up immediately in someone's workflow. That's the kind of feedback loop I've gotten the most out of. When I built ClarityAtlas, an iOS health app I shipped into a live pilot at Johns Hopkins with 30+ users, the hard part wasn't the passive sleep-estimation system I built on HealthKit and Core Motion data, it was finding out which parts of it people actually understood and used. I'm also drawn to Hudl because sports data is a messy, high-volume, real-time problem, and most of my work has been on systems where the modeling is only useful if the output is something a human can act on, like the anomaly-detection and alert-prioritization work I do now. Mostly I want to own a real piece of a production system and learn from engineers who have been maintaining one for years.",
